  Agent guidance:
  Core spring-data-couchbase has no Spring Boot autoconfiguration, so the test must wire a CouchbaseClientFactory/Cluster manually (e.g. extend AbstractCouchbaseConfiguration or build SimpleCouchbaseClientFactory directly) using the container's getConnectionString()/getUsername()/getPassword(). Use a BucketDefinition with withPrimaryIndex(true) so derived N1QL query methods (findByXxx) have an index to run against. Call cluster.waitUntilReady(timeout) before issuing repository operations to avoid flaky early access. Start the container once per test class via a static @Container field (not per-test) so the ~30-90s cluster/bucket/index bootstrap is amortized and individual @Test methods stay within the repo's 60-second budget. required-docker-images.txt must also list testcontainers/ryuk:0.14.0 (the exact tag already pinned in allowed-docker-images/Dockerfile-testcontainers_ryuk), matching the pattern used by the existing microcks-testcontainers test.
  
  Risks:
  - Couchbase Server containers are heavier and slower to initialize than typical TCK Docker images (often 30-90s for cluster/bucket/index setup), which could strain the per-test 60s and overall suite-time budgets even when amortized across a shared container.
  - Exact image tag and testcontainers-couchbase/testcontainers-junit-jupiter versions need re-verification at generation time -- the 2.x 'testcontainers-' artifact rename is recent and I could not confirm current Maven Central coordinates against a live resolver in this sandbox.
